    {
      "id": "0e32c541-b73f-4d75-a070-44edf797dce8",
      "name": "Sticky Note 32f1aecb",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -432,
        64
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 4,
        "width": 596,
        "height": 668,
        "content": "## \ud83c\udf19 Maintenance ON \u2014 Nightly Workflow Shutdown\n\nDeactivates every currently **active** workflow on this n8n instance on a schedule, then logs what it turned off so a companion **\"Maintenance OFF\"** workflow can bring everything back the next morning.\n\n### What it does\n1. Fires on a schedule (default 22:30)\n2. Fetches all active workflows via the n8n API\n3. Skips itself and any workflow you list as excluded\n4. Deactivates each remaining workflow\n5. Logs workflow_id, workflow_name and deactivated_at to a Data Table, so \"Maintenance OFF\" knows exactly what to turn back on\n\n### You need\n- An **n8n API key** (Settings \u2192 n8n API), added as credentials on both HTTP Request nodes\n- A **Data Table** with columns workflow_id (string), workflow_name (string), deactivated_at (date/time)\n- A companion **\"Maintenance OFF\"** workflow that reads that table and reactivates the listed workflows (not included in this template)\n\n### Before you activate\n1. Open **Config** and set base_url to your own instance URL\n2. In excluded_workflow_ids, list \u2014 comma-separated \u2014 the workflow IDs to never touch (this workflow excludes itself automatically)\n3. Connect your n8n API credential on both HTTP Request nodes\n4. Pick or create your Data Table on **Save to maintenance_log**\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f The \"fetch active workflows\" call uses limit=250. With more than 250 active workflows, add pagination (nextCursor)."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ec732044-14d5-4e53-b78a-5cb47ea1583c",
      "name": "Note - Trigger & Config",
